The matrix is processed one layer at a time. For each layer, the code reads the top edge, right edge, bottom edge, and left edge. The bottom and left edges are skipped when the layer has collapsed to one row or one column.

Using `layer` makes it clear which outer ring is being handled. The input list is only read, not changed.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public List<int> SpiralOrder(List<List<int>> matrix) {
        List<int> result = new List<int>();
        if (matrix == null || matrix.Count == 0 || matrix[0].Count == 0) {
            return result;
        }

        int rows = matrix.Count;
        int columns = matrix[0].Count;
        int layer = 0;

        while (layer * 2 < rows && layer * 2 < columns) {
            int top = layer;
            int bottom = rows - 1 - layer;
            int left = layer;
            int right = columns - 1 - layer;

            for (int column = left; column <= right; ++column) {
                result.Add(matrix[top][column]);
            }

            for (int row = top + 1; row <= bottom; ++row) {
                result.Add(matrix[row][right]);
            }

            if (top < bottom) {
                for (int column = right - 1; column >= left; --column) {
                    result.Add(matrix[bottom][column]);
                }
            }

            if (left < right) {
                for (int row = bottom - 1; row > top; --row) {
                    result.Add(matrix[row][left]);
                }
            }

            ++layer;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

For a single row such as `[[7, 8, 9]]`, the top edge adds all three values and the other edge checks prevent duplicates. A 1 by 1 matrix also goes through one layer and returns its only value.

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned list.

Kept the traversal around the current rectangle. The four values `top`, `bottom`, `left`, and `right` describe the part that is not read yet. After visiting one side, its boundary moves inward.

The checks before the bottom row and left column are important for a single row or a single column.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> spiralOrder(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {
        vector<int> result;
        if (matrix.empty() || matrix[0].empty()) {
            return result;
        }

        int top = 0;
        int bottom = static_cast<int>(matrix.size()) - 1;
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(matrix[0].size()) - 1;

        while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {
            for (int column = left; column <= right; ++column) {
                result.push_back(matrix[top][column]);
            }
            ++top;

            for (int row = top; row <= bottom; ++row) {
                result.push_back(matrix[row][right]);
            }
            --right;

            if (top <= bottom) {
                for (int column = right; column >= left; --column) {
                    result.push_back(matrix[bottom][column]);
                }
                --bottom;
            }

            if (left <= right) {
                for (int row = bottom; row >= top; --row) {
                    result.push_back(matrix[row][left]);
                }
                ++left;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

For `[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]`, the first round adds `1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 8, 7, 4`. The boundaries then leave only `5`.

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned vector. The input matrix is not changed.

Instead of storing four changing boundaries, this version stores how many cells remain on the next horizontal and vertical sides. The side lengths shrink after each pass. The direction list keeps the order right, down, left, up.

For a 3 by 4 matrix, the first horizontal side has length 4, the first vertical side has length 2, and the next horizontal side has length 3. That pattern continues until no side has cells left.

```go
func spiralOrder(matrix [][]int) []int {
    if len(matrix) == 0 || len(matrix[0]) == 0 {
        return []int{}
    }

    rows := len(matrix)
    columns := len(matrix[0])
    result := make([]int, 0, rows*columns)

    directions := [4][2]int{
        {0, 1},
        {1, 0},
        {0, -1},
        {-1, 0},
    }

    steps := [2]int{columns, rows - 1}
    direction := 0
    row, column := 0, -1

    for steps[direction%2] > 0 {
        length := steps[direction%2]
        for count := 0; count < length; count++ {
            row += directions[direction][0]
            column += directions[direction][1]
            result = append(result, matrix[row][column])
        }

        steps[direction%2]--
        direction = (direction + 1) % 4
    }

    return result
}
```

A quick dry run for `[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]` uses lengths `3, 1, 2, 0`. The values are read as `1, 2, 3`, then `6`, then `5, 4`, and the zero length stops the loop.

The starting column is `-1` because the first move is to the right before reading a value. The method does not change the input matrix.

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned slice.

Here the movement is controlled by four directions: right, down, left, and up. A boolean grid records visited cells. Whenever the next cell is outside the matrix or already used, the direction turns clockwise.

This avoids boundary calculations, but the visited grid costs extra memory. The loop runs exactly once for every cell.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> spiralOrder(int[][] matrix) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (matrix == null || matrix.length == 0 || matrix[0].length == 0) {
            return result;
        }

        int rows = matrix.length;
        int columns = matrix[0].length;
        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][columns];
        int[][] directions = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {-1, 0}};

        int row = 0;
        int column = 0;
        int direction = 0;

        for (int count = 0; count < rows * columns; ++count) {
            result.add(matrix[row][column]);
            visited[row][column] = true;

            int nextRow = row + directions[direction][0];
            int nextColumn = column + directions[direction][1];

            if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                nextColumn < 0 || nextColumn >= columns ||
                visited[nextRow][nextColumn]) {
                direction = (direction + 1) % 4;
                nextRow = row + directions[direction][0];
                nextColumn = column + directions[direction][1];
            }

            row = nextRow;
            column = nextColumn;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

For a one-column matrix such as `[[4], [5], [6]]`, the first move goes down until the last cell, then the direction changes. No cell is repeated. An empty matrix also returns an empty list before `matrix[0]` is accessed.

Time complexity: `O(rows * columns)`.

Space complexity: `O(rows * columns)` for `visited`, not counting the result list. The input matrix stays unchanged.

